Consolidated Interview Q&A (important questions + short answers)

1) What is a VPC — short definition

VPC (Virtual Private Cloud) = logically isolated virtual network in AWS where you launch AWS resources (EC2, RDS, EKS, etc.).

You control IP address range (CIDR), subnets, route tables, network gateways, ACLs, security groups, endpoints.

Analogy: VPC = your private datacenter network in AWS.

2) Create First VPC — step-by-step
Console steps (quick)

AWS Console → VPC → Your VPCs → Create VPC.

Name tag: my-first-vpc

IPv4 CIDR block: e.g. 10.0.0.0/16 (gives 65k addresses)

IPv6: optional (assign Amazon-provided IPv6 CIDR)

Tenancy: default (shared) or dedicated (rare).

Create subnets (next step) and IGW if you want internet.

AWS CLI (create VPC)
# create vpc
aws ec2 create-vpc --cidr-block 10.0.0.0/16 --tag-specifications 'ResourceType=vpc,Tags=[{Key=Name,Value=my-first-vpc}]'

# note the VpcId from output (e.g., vpc-0abc...)

3) Create First Subnet
Concepts

Subnet = subdivision of VPC CIDR in a specific AZ (AZ-scoped).

Use multiple AZs for HA.

Example design (small lab)

VPC 10.0.0.0/16

Public subnet AZ-a: 10.0.1.0/24

Private subnet AZ-a: 10.0.2.0/24

Public subnet AZ-b: 10.0.3.0/24

Private subnet AZ-b: 10.0.4.0/24

Console steps

VPC → Subnets → Create subnet → choose VPC, AZ, CIDR, name.

CLI example
aws ec2 create-subnet --vpc-id vpc-0abc123 --cidr-block 10.0.1.0/24 --availability-zone ap-south-1a --tag-specifications 'ResourceType=subnet,Tags=[{Key=Name,Value=public-a}]'

4) Custom VPC & Subnet without IGW and Route Table (isolated/air-gapped)

Create a VPC and subnets but do not attach an Internet Gateway — instances will be private & no internet.

Useful for sensitive workloads or on-prem connectivity only (VPN/Direct Connect).

Make route table local-only (default route local exists automatically). Don’t add 0.0.0.0/0 route.

Steps

Create VPC & subnets.

Do not create IGW.

Launch EC2 in subnet — it will have private IP only; no internet unless NAT/VPN present.

5) Internet Gateway (IGW) & Route Table — how they work + setup
What is IGW?

IGW = horizontally scaled, redundant Amazon-managed gateway that allows communication between instances in your VPC and the internet.

IGW is region-wide and must be attached to VPC.

Route Table basics

A route table holds routes (destination CIDR → target).

Default local route 10.0.0.0/16 → local exists automatically.

To make a subnet public: route 0.0.0.0/0 → igw-id in the subnet’s associated route table.

Steps: Make subnet public

Create IGW: Console VPC → Internet Gateways → Create → Attach to VPC.

Route table → select route table associated with public subnet (or create new RT) → Edit routes → Add route: Destination 0.0.0.0/0 Target igw-xxxxx.

Launch EC2 in that subnet and assign a public IP (auto-assign or Elastic IP). Ensure Security Group allows inbound SSH/HTTP as required.

CLI (create IGW + route)
aws ec2 create-internet-gateway
aws ec2 attach-internet-gateway --vpc-id vpc-0abc123 --internet-gateway-id igw-0abc
aws ec2 create-route --route-table-id rtb-0abc --destination-cidr-block 0.0.0.0/0 --gateway-id igw-0abc

6) Public vs Private Subnet — design patterns
Definitions

Public subnet: subnet with a route to IGW and instances have public IPs or Elastic IPs → reachable from internet (subject to SG rules).

Private subnet: no direct route to IGW. Instances have only private IPs. Internet access via NAT (if required).

Typical 3-tier VPC pattern

Public subnets: load balancers, bastion host, NAT gateway.

Private subnets: application servers, databases.

DB subnet group: private subnets across AZs (for RDS Multi-AZ).

Example CIDR plan

10.0.0.0/16

Public-A 10.0.1.0/24

Private-A 10.0.2.0/24

Public-B 10.0.3.0/24

Private-B 10.0.4.0/24

7) Access Private Instance using Bastion Host (jump box)
When to use

You need SSH/RDP into private instances without public IP.

Design

Place bastion in public subnet, harden it (SG limited to your IP), use it to SSH to private instance.

Steps (Console)

Launch EC2 in public subnet (bastion) with public IP. Harden: SG allows SSH only from your office/IP.

Launch target EC2 in private subnet (no public IP).

SSH: ssh -i bastion.pem ec2-user@<bastion-public-ip> then from bastion ssh -i private_key.pem ec2-user@10.0.2.10 (private IP).

Optionally enable SSH agent forwarding or set ProxyCommand in ~/.ssh/config:

Host bastion
  Hostname bastion-public-ip
  User ec2-user
  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/bastion.pem

Host private*
  User ec2-user
  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/private.pem
  ProxyCommand ssh -W %h:%p bastion

More secure: Session Manager (SSM)

Use AWS Systems Manager Session Manager to avoid bastion entirely (attach IAM role & SSM agent) — recommended.

8) Private Instance Internet Access — NAT Instance
What is NAT instance?

A user-managed EC2 instance that performs NAT for private subnets (source NAT). Older pattern before NAT Gateway.

How to set up (high-level)

Launch a dedicated EC2 (Amazon Linux) in public subnet. Enable Source/Dest check = disabled.

Add route in private subnet route table: 0.0.0.0/0 → nat-instance-private-ip.

Security groups: NAT instance allows outbound; private instances route via NAT.

Commands (disable source/dest check)
aws ec2 modify-instance-attribute --instance-id i-012345 --no-source-dest-check

Pros

Cheap for very low traffic, customizable.

Cons

Single point of failure unless you build HA (auto-failover scripts), management overhead, scaling, maintenance.

9) Private Instance Internet Access — NAT Gateway (recommended)
What is NAT Gateway?

AWS-managed, highly available NAT service per AZ; scales automatically.

Setup steps

Create NAT Gateway in public subnet (requires Elastic IP).

Update route table for private subnets: 0.0.0.0/0 → nat-gateway-id.

Private instances send internet-bound traffic to NAT gateway (SNAT) and get internet access.

CLI
aws ec2 create-nat-gateway --subnet-id subnet-0abc --allocation-id eipalloc-01234

Pros

Managed, highly available, scales well (per AZ), easier than NAT instance.

Cons

Cost: hourly + data processing charges. For high egress, costs add up.

Best practice

Deploy one NAT gateway per AZ and associate private subnets in that AZ to avoid cross-AZ charges.

10) NACL — Network ACL (stateless firewall), NACL vs Security Group
What is NACL?

Subnet-level network access control list that allows/denies inbound/outbound traffic. Stateless (must configure both directions). Evaluated by rule number order (lowest first) and supports allow & deny.

Security Group (SG)

Instance-level, stateful (reply allowed automatically), allows only allow rules, applies to instance ENI.

Differences
Feature	Security Group	NACL
Level	Instance	Subnet
Stateful?	Yes	No
Rules	Allow only	Allow & Deny
Evaluated	All SGs applied	Rule number order
Use-case	Primary protection	Extra layer, blacklist/whitelist, compliance
When to use NACL?

Additional subnet-level filtering, e.g., block a suspicious IP across subnet regardless of SG. But SG is usually sufficient.

11) Create NACL — step-by-step
Console

VPC → Network ACLs → Create network ACL → Choose VPC.

Edit inbound/outbound rules (rule number, protocol, port range, allow/deny, source/destination).

Associate NACL to subnet (can associate to multiple; each subnet only one NACL).

Example: Allow HTTP/HTTPS and SSH from specific IP

Inbound rule 100: Allow TCP 80 0.0.0.0/0

Inbound rule 110: Allow TCP 443 0.0.0.0/0

Inbound rule 120: Allow TCP 22 <your-ip>/32

Outbound rule 100: Allow all traffic 0.0.0.0/0
Remember to add reverse outbound allow rules if necessary (because stateless).

12) VPC Cleanup — best practices & steps
Why cleanup?

Avoid unexpected costs (NAT gateways, EIPs), remove stale resources, security.

Checklist to clean VPC

Terminate EC2 instances.

Delete ENIs (Elastic Network Interfaces) if stuck.

Release Elastic IP addresses (EIP).

Delete NAT Gateways (and associated EIPs).

Delete Route Tables (custom), detach IGW then delete IGW.

Delete Subnets.

Delete Security Groups (can't delete default SG) — remove dependencies.

Delete NACLs (if custom).

Delete VPC.

CLI example (detach and delete IGW)
aws ec2 detach-internet-gateway --internet-gateway-id igw-0123 --vpc-id vpc-0123
aws ec2 delete-internet-gateway --internet-gateway-id igw-0123


Be careful with dependencies — deletion order matters.

13) VPC Peering — concepts, limitations, setup
What is VPC Peering?

Private network connection between two VPCs (same or different accounts/regions with Inter-Region Peering support) allowing routing via private IPs.

Key points

Peering is non-transitive (A↔B and B↔C does not imply A↔C).

Works cross-account.

No single-hop routing through a third VPC.

No overlapping CIDRs allowed (must be unique).

You update route tables to route traffic to peer vpc via peering connection.

Use-cases

App in one VPC talking to DB in another account VPC.

Shared services VPC (logging, authentication).

Setup (Console)

VPC A → Peering Connections → Create Peering Connection → Choose remote VPC (same account or other account).

Accept in the remote account (if cross-account).

Update route tables: In VPC A route table: peerCIDR → pcx-xxxxx; In VPC B route: peerCIDR → pcx-xxxxx.

Update Security Groups to allow traffic from peer range.

CLI example
aws ec2 create-vpc-peering-connection --vpc-id vpc-aaa --peer-vpc-id vpc-bbb --peer-owner-id 123456789012
aws ec2 accept-vpc-peering-connection --vpc-peering-connection-id pcx-012345

14) Transit Gateway (TGW) — intro, attachments, use-cases
What is Transit Gateway?

Central hub to connect many VPCs, on-prem networks (via VGW), and VPNs. Replaces mesh of peering with hub-and-spoke.

Benefits

Scales to thousands of VPCs across accounts/regions (with TGW Inter-Region).

Supports route propagation, attachments, central routing policies.

Attachments

VPC Attachment: attach a VPC to TGW (specify subnet in each AZ to create ENIs).

VPN Attachment: connect customer gateway to TGW.

Direct Connect Gateway attachment possible.

High-level steps

Create Transit Gateway (Console → Transit Gateway → Create).

Create attachment: VPC → Actions → Create Transit Gateway Attachment.

Accept attachment on TGW side if needed.

Route: update TGW route table and VPC route table to send traffic to TGW via attachment.

CLI example
aws ec2 create-transit-gateway --description "MyTGW"
aws ec2 create-transit-gateway-vpc-attachment --transit-gateway-id tgw-0abc --vpc-id vpc-0123 --subnet-ids subnet-1 subnet-2

Use-case patterns

Multi-account networking at scale.

Centralized security & inspection (via inspection VPC).

On-prem connectivity consolidation.

15) Transit Gateway — Part 2 (advanced)
Considerations

Route table per TGW — you can create multiple TGW route tables (segmentation).

Propagation: attachments can propagate routes automatically.

Security: use Security Groups & NACLs in VPCs; TGW itself is not a firewall.

Inter-region TGW peering for global connectivity (separate charges).

Cost: TGW hourly + data processing per GB.

Design tip

Use one TGW with route tables per environment (prod/dev), attach only required VPCs to route table.

Use Transit VPC for inspection: forward traffic to inspection EC2s (security appliances) via TGW route table.

16) Delete Transit Gateway & clean up
Steps

Detach all attachments (VPC attachments, VPN, DX).

Delete TGW attachments.

Delete Transit Gateway.

Remove route table entries & dependencies.

CLI detach example:

aws ec2 delete-transit-gateway-vpc-attachment --transit-gateway-attachment-id tgw-attach-0abc
aws ec2 delete-transit-gateway --transit-gateway-id tgw-01234


Be careful: attachments must be cleared and acceptance statuses resolved.

17) VPC Flow Logs — what, how, analyze, costs
What are Flow Logs?

Capture IP traffic metadata (accept/reject, src/dst, ports, protocol) for ENIs, subnets or VPCs. Data sent to CloudWatch Logs or S3.

Use-cases

Security troubleshooting, forensic analysis, traffic accounting, audit.

Create (Console)

VPC → Your VPC → Create flow log → Choose resource (VPC/subnet/ENI), destination (CloudWatch Logs or S3), filter (All / Accept / Reject), IAM role.

Example (CLI)
aws ec2 create-flow-logs --resource-ids vpc-0123 --resource-type VPC --traffic-type ALL --log-group-name vpc-flowlogs --deliver-logs-permission-arn arn:aws:iam::123456:role/flow-logs-role

Log fields (version 2)

version, account-id, interface-id, srcaddr, dstaddr, srcport, dstport, protocol, packets, bytes, start, end, action, log-status

Analysis

Use Athena (if logs in S3) to query.

Use CloudWatch Insights (if in CloudWatch).

Export to SIEM (Splunk) for correlation.

Cost

CloudWatch ingestion costs and S3 storage costs. Keep sampling/filters to control cost.

18) VPC Endpoints — Gateway Endpoint (S3 & DynamoDB)
What is Gateway Endpoint?

A routeTable-target endpoint for S3 or DynamoDB enabling VPC resources to privately access S3/DDB without internet/IGW/NAT.

Benefits

No internet egress, improved security, better performance for S3 traffic.

Setup (Console)

VPC → Endpoints → Create Endpoint.

Service category: AWS services → Choose com.amazonaws.<region>.s3 (Gateway).

Select VPC, and route tables to modify (the route tables used by subnets).

Optionally add bucket policy to allow access only from VPCE.

CLI
aws ec2 create-vpc-endpoint --vpc-id vpc-0123 --service-name com.amazonaws.ap-south-1.s3 --route-table-ids rtb-0123 rtb-0456

Use-case

EC2 in private subnet writes to S3 without NAT gateway.

19) Interface Endpoint — AWS PrivateLink
What is Interface Endpoint?

Elastic Network Interface (ENI) in your subnet that provides private connectivity to many AWS services and partner services (via PrivateLink). Does not use route tables.

Services supported

Many AWS services (e.g., CloudWatch Logs, ECR, SSM), AWS Marketplace/partner services.

Steps (Console)

VPC → Endpoints → Create endpoint → Service category AWS services → choose service (com.amazonaws.region.logs) or PrivateLink partner.

Select subnets (ENIs created in them) and security groups to control access.

Use-cases

Private communication to service APIs without public internet.

CLI
aws ec2 create-vpc-endpoint --vpc-id vpc-0123 --service-name com.amazonaws.ap-south-1.ec2 --vpc-endpoint-type Interface --subnet-ids subnet-1 subnet-2 --security-group-ids sg-0abc


Note: Interface endpoints have hourly and data processing charges.

20) Site-to-Site VPN & Virtual Private Gateway
Concept

Connect on-prem datacenter to AWS VPC over IPsec VPN (customer gateway ↔ virtual private gateway). Supports dynamic routing (BGP) or static routes.

Components

Customer Gateway (CGW): your on-prem device public IP.

Virtual Private Gateway (VGW): AWS-side VPN gateway attached to VPC.

VPN Connection: IPsec tunnels (usually two for redundancy).

Steps (high-level)

Create Customer Gateway in AWS (specify on-prem public IP).

Create Virtual Private Gateway (VPC → VPN) and Attach to VPC.

Create VPN Connection between CGW and VGW (download VPN config for your vendor).

Update route tables to route on-prem CIDR via VGW.

On-prem device configure tunnels (use downloaded config).

CLI example
aws ec2 create-vpn-connection --type ipsec.1 --customer-gateway-id cgw-0123 --vpn-gateway-id vgw-0123 --options StaticRoutesOnly=true

Use-cases

Hybrid cloud, lift & shift, database replication.

21) Egress-Only Internet Gateway (for IPv6)
Purpose

For IPv6-only instances to have outbound-only access to the internet while preventing inbound (unsolicited) IPv6 traffic.

Key points

IPv6 uses Egress-Only IGW; IPv4 uses NAT/IGW.

Attach Egress-Only IGW to VPC and add route ::/0 to it in private subnet route table.

Create (Console)

VPC → Egress-Only Internet Gateways → Create Egress-Only IGW → Attach to VPC.

CLI:

aws ec2 create-egress-only-internet-gateway --vpc-id vpc-0123

22) Consolidated Interview Q&A (Top VPC Questions)

Q: What is a VPC?
A: Virtual Private Cloud – isolated virtual network in AWS.

Q: How to make subnet public?
A: Attach IGW to VPC and add route 0.0.0.0/0 → igw in route table; ensure instances have public IP/EIP.

Q: NAT Instance vs NAT Gateway — difference?
A: NAT instance = self-managed EC2 with source/dest check disabled; NAT Gateway = managed AWS service, scalable, per-AZ, chargeable. NAT GW recommended.

Q: Security Group vs NACL?
A: SG = instance-level, stateful, allow-only. NACL = subnet-level, stateless, allows deny/allow.

Q: What is VPC Peering?
A: Private connection between two VPCs; non-transitive; needs route table entries.

Q: When to use Transit Gateway?
A: For connecting many VPCs at scale (hub-and-spoke) and consolidating on-prem connections.

Q: What are VPC Endpoints?
A: Private connectivity to AWS services: Gateway endpoint (S3/DDB) modifies route tables; Interface endpoints (PrivateLink) create ENIs in subnets.

Q: Why use Flow Logs?
A: To capture traffic metadata for security, audit, troubleshooting.

Q: How to access private instance without bastion?
A: Use AWS Systems Manager Session Manager (SSM) — recommended because no SSH keys/public IPs needed.

Q: Can you peer VPCs with overlapping CIDR?
A: No — CIDRs must not overlap for peering.

Q: Is TGW transitive?
A: TGW supports routing multiple VPCs via TGW — it’s effectively hub and can route (controlled by TGW route tables), but peering different TGWs is different. Transit Gateway supports transitive routing among attachments.

Practical tips, best practices & cost notes

Subnet sizing: leave headroom; avoid /28 small subnets.

AZ design: spread across AZs for HA (ELB, ASG, DB Multi-AZ).

NAT Gateway: create per AZ so private subnet traffic stays AZ-local (avoid cross-AZ charges).

Security posture: default deny inbound in SGs; restrict egress if required.

SSM over Bastion: prefer SSM Session Manager for secure, auditable remote access without opening SSH.

Use Endpoints to remove need for NAT for S3/DDB access (reduces NAT egress costs).

Flow Logs costs: choose S3 + Athena for long-term storage and analysis to save CloudWatch ingestion costs.

VPC Peering vs TGW: use peering for small number of VPCs/simple cases; use TGW at scale.

IPv6: if using IPv6, use Egress-Only IGW for outbound-only behavior.

